# BUG-309 — a regex OOM used to be reported as "no match".
#
# `matchAt` is typed `error{OutOfMemory}!?usize`, so OOM is its ONLY failure, and
# find/findAll/replace each discarded it into `null`: a failed check read as a passed one.
# Fixed to `catch @panic("regex: out of memory")`, matching what the very next line in each
# of those functions already does for its own output buffer.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE CANNOT DO, stated plainly: it does not reproduce the bug. Forcing an
# allocation failure inside the engine is not reachable from Zebra source. This is a
# CONTROL on the fix, not a reproduction. What it does prove is the half that a wrong fix
# would break -- that a GENUINE no-match still returns empty and does not panic. Those two
# outcomes were indistinguishable before; the whole point of the fix is that they are not
# indistinguishable now, and this pins the benign one.
def main()
    var re: Regex = Regex.compile("\\d+")

    # A real match.
    if re.find("abc 42 def") <> "42"
        print("FAIL: find")
        return

    # A real MISS. This is the important leg: the fix must not have turned "no match"
    # into a panic.
    if re.find("no digits here") <> ""
        print("FAIL: genuine no-match must stay empty")
        return

    # replace still substitutes.
    if re.replace("a1b22c", "#") <> "a#b#c"
        print("FAIL: replace")
        return

    print("bug309: OK")
